# Sample crontab.
#
# cronplain reads five-field expressions, so take the first five columns and feed
# them in one per line. The sixth column of a system crontab is the user the job
# runs as, which cronplain does not care about:
#
#   awk '/^[^#]/ && NF >= 5 {print $1, $2, $3, $4, $5}' examples/crontab.sample | cronplain -
#
# (awk rather than `cut -d' '`, because the columns below are aligned with spaces
#  and cut would emit empty fields.)

# --- environment ------------------------------------------------------------
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
MAILTO=root

# --- regular maintenance ----------------------------------------------------
0 6 * * * root /usr/local/sbin/backup-daily
30 6 * * 0 root /usr/local/sbin/backup-weekly
*/5 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/healthcheck --quiet
15 2 1 * * root /usr/local/sbin/rotate-logs

# --- business hours ---------------------------------------------------------
*/15 9-17 * * MON-FRI app /usr/local/bin/sync-calendar
0 22 * * 5 app /usr/local/bin/weekly-digest

# --- the classic mistake ----------------------------------------------------
# Almost certainly written to mean "the first Monday of the month". It does not
# mean that. Day-of-month and day-of-week are unioned, so this fires on the 1st
# of every month *and* on every Monday — roughly sixty-four runs a year, not
# twelve. cronplain warns about it:
#
#   $ cronplain "0 3 1 * MON"
#   At 00:00... (see the warning on stderr)
#
0 3 1 * MON app /usr/local/bin/monthly-report

# --- and the honest fix -----------------------------------------------------
# Pure cron cannot express "first Monday" portably. `1-7 * MON` is frequently
# recommended, but it only means "the first Monday" in implementations that
# intersect the two day fields; Vixie cron, which most Linux distributions ship,
# unions them, so that line fires on days 1-7 *and* on every Monday.
#
# The reliable form keeps a plain weekly trigger and lets the command decide:
0 3 * * MON app /usr/local/bin/monthly-report --if-first-monday
